// result_end

In the example the interaction is not significant, so it is pooled into repeatability. Operators differ slightly (\(F=4.0\), \(p=0.02\)), but their variance is small. The measurement system takes 21% of the study variation and 20% of the tolerance, almost all of it repeatability, the gauge itself. It resolves six distinct categories of parts. By the AIAG rules this gage is marginal: acceptable for some uses, and the place to improve is the equipment, not operator training.

The simulation shows how noisy that verdict is. The usual design has 10 parts, 3 operators and 2 replicates. With a true %GRR of 20%, comfortably inside the "marginal" band, one study in ten still reports more than 30% and calls the gage unacceptable; the middle 80% of estimates span 14–30%. A third replicate barely helps (14.6–29.9) because repeatability is already well estimated. What limits precision is the few operators and parts. With 20 parts the range narrows to 15–26%, and with 6 operators the operator variance is no longer estimated as zero 12% of the time. The small 5 × 2 × 2 design is nearly useless: its estimates range from 12% to 39%, and their distribution is right-skewed (mean 23.7% against a median near 20%).

>> Practical guidance

- Choose parts that span the real process range, not hand-picked good parts. %study variation and the number of distinct categories depend on the part variation in the study. Use %tolerance when the gage is for conformance decisions rather than process control.
- Use at least 10 parts and 3 operators. When reproducibility matters or the verdict is close to a limit, add parts and operators rather than replicates. Report the estimate together with its uncertainty (confidence intervals for variance components, for example by the modified large-sample method), not just the acceptability category.
- Check the ANOVA table. A significant operator-by-part interaction means operators disagree in part-specific ways (fixturing, reading technique), and it should be investigated, not just pooled away.
- Randomize the measurement order and blind operators to part identity. Otherwise memory and drift inflate or hide repeatability.

>> References

- Automotive Industry Action Group (2010). *Measurement Systems Analysis Reference Manual*, 4th ed. AIAG.
- R. K. Burdick, C. M. Borror and D. C. Montgomery (2005). *Design and Analysis of Gauge R&R Studies: Making Decisions with Confidence Intervals in Random and Mixed ANOVA Models*. ASA-SIAM. doi:10.1137/1.9780898718379
- D. C. Montgomery and G. C. Runger (1993). Gauge capability and designed experiments. Part I: Basic methods. *Quality Engineering* 6(1), 115-135. doi:10.1080/08982119308918710
- S. R. Searle, G. Casella and C. E. McCulloch (1992). *Variance Components*. Wiley. doi:10.1002/9780470316856
